#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247103 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064253 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b492068c-2bd2-5611-baab-780959d24c3d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function Using a Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0de97e9b-167d-58b8-b3d5-94d7072f96fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244438} {:answer/id #uuid "411a9d60-45d9-5255-af7c-f54d7ac9a709" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244886} {:answer/id #uuid "7480aff9-21a8-5cfa-9c98-bc986f215b03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{31}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245351} {:answer/id #uuid "a3928f90-c565-5339-83c1-14d12d515196" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{44}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245773} {:answer/id #uuid "e8d9aa97-30ab-5be0-9392-8e2eebc6259c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246375}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7480aff9-21a8-5cfa-9c98-bc986f215b03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{31}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245351} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f27b98d-efdc-5fef-8552-5c0790435d8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246742}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "32e90e8e-86b1-556f-b1e0-fc9bc969a8b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-110284" :question/problem "The following table gives the values of $f(x),{f}^{′}(x),g(x),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ at some points. Find $({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(3)$ of the product of functions.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $2$ | $-4$ | $-1$ | $-3$ |\n| $-2$ | $3$ | $-5$ | $-4$ | $2$ |" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function ${f}^{-1}(x)g(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(x)=\\frac{g(x)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}+{g}^{′}(x){f}^{-1}(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(3)=\\frac{g(3)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(3))}+{g}^{′}(3){f}^{-1}(3).\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(3)=-2,$ $g(3)=-1,$ ${g}^{′}(3)=-3,$ and ${f}^{′}(-2)=-5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(3) & =\\frac{g(3)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(3))}+{g}^{′}(3){f}^{-1}(3) \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{{f}^{′}(-2)}+(-3)\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{-5}+6 \\\\  & =\\frac{31}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247110 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064254 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b2a80aa-7882-50eb-bfe0-c0eeab72161d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Derivative Using Properties of Inverse Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d86fb7c-04a2-5054-88db-d87318f91df0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245716} {:answer/id #uuid "a7efd507-c58e-56ec-8841-05d7624ba7c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245816} {:answer/id #uuid "b538f1e2-5740-5e86-9585-792990790c3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245930} {:answer/id #uuid "cf18104f-33a0-52e9-88d9-69f208eb80ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246163} {:answer/id #uuid "f4626bee-994c-50c4-b9f0-fb3dfa085a3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246468}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f4626bee-994c-50c4-b9f0-fb3dfa085a3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246468} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b79bcdf-534b-5594-b18b-23bc8c71894d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246735}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "697e17f6-464a-5495-90d8-1dc0fdda0186" :question/math-academy-id "q-110338" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{x+10}{x+1}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(4).$" :question/worked-solution "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(4),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(4)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(4).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(4)=x.$ For $x\\ne -1,$ this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(4) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =4 \\\\ \\frac{x+10}{x+1} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{x+10}{x+1}\\cdot (x+1) & =4\\cdot (x+1) \\\\ \\frac{x+10}{\\cancel{(x+1)}}\\cdot \\cancel{(x+1)} & =4x+4 \\\\ x+10 & =4x+4 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(4)=2.$\n\nNow we calculate ${f}^{′}(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x+10}{x+1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{9}{(x+1)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =-\\frac{9}{{(2+1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{{(3)}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{9} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(4) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
